    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_263a4a7dc9193487ed533ef641d40c061037479e1cb7631055896ae4376e12f0",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Modified PT symmetry and self-adjoint extension theory provide different kinds of spectral control. For the difference Hamiltonian, PT constrains the formal eigenvalues of iH to the real axis or conjugate pairs but does not provide a domain or self-adjointness. In the separate half-line construction, selecting Neumann or Dirichlet extensions of momentum squared yields complete cosine or sine bases in which log absolute momentum is defined. The comparison isolates domain selection and completeness as additional mechanisms beyond symmetry-imposed pairing.",
