    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_31459a78affcbe81ab20839a53f2b05516c5a7db91783c96b067edf97e7dcf03",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The Lie-Noether construction and the difference Hamiltonian expose distinct stages of a spectral program. In the first, symmetry reduction fixes two temporal exponents but leaves arbitrary functions of the reduced coordinate; in the second, formal eigenstates have not been placed on a common domain supporting the boundary condition, true adjoint, completeness, and self-adjointness. The reusable comparison is that local reduction and formal spectral identities do not by themselves supply the global admissibility data needed for a discrete operator spectrum.",
