    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_71c87673be066a6f74633b1add983de061a4f4beb92abb4ad3ddc86a09d87584",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The two dynamical routes produce different mathematical objects. In the Lorentz-gas route, primitive lattice-horizon sums acquire inverse-zeta singularities after Mellin transformation, and the claimed zero-sensitive remainder still depends on an unproved contour-closing estimate. In the flow route, graph-diagonal transversality is verified and the fixed-point distribution with return-map determinant weights is derived. The reusable comparison is that pole bookkeeping after a scalar transform is not a distributional trace identity or an operator spectrum.",
