    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_a63d2b8b8ec5adececf27624ada8b0b2d067082ab1108651c20634ab8801f138",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The phase-space inverse problem gives a concrete answer-encoding mechanism: a prescribed fluctuation density determines boundary displacement and, in the monotone regime, reconstructs the boundary curve. Connes's naturality criterion excludes defining a Hilbert-Polya pair from the zeros it is meant to explain. The cyclic-RG tests provide a forward contrast: the examined inverse-power level shifts and prime-level deletions retain smooth arctangent or multi-gamma responses and do not reproduce the irregular zero locations.",
